== 0. Seeley-DeWitt data of S^3, S^4 from explicit curvature tensors (Gilkey; Vassilevich arXiv:hep-th/0306138)
   S^3 (unit): R = 6, |Ric|^2 = 12, |Riem|^2 = 12
   S^4 (unit): R = 12, |Ric|^2 = 36, |Riem|^2 = 24
PASS 0a curvature invariants of the unit S^d: R = d(d-1), |Ric|^2 = d(d-1)^2, |Riem|^2 = 2d(d-1)  (d = 3, 4, from the metric)
   Gilkey for -Lap:  S^3: a_1 = 1, a_2 = 1/2 ;  S^4: a_1 = 2, a_2 = 29/15   (units L^-2, L^-4)
   integrated (4 pi)^(-2) Vol a_2:  minimal scalar 29/90 ; conformal scalar -1/90
PASS 0b Gilkey: S^3 a_1 = 1, a_2 = 1/2 (= 1/n! pattern); S^4 minimal scalar (4 pi)^-2 Vol a_2 = 29/90; conformal scalar = -1/90

== 1. S^3 (unit radius; eps = b/L^2):  T3(eps) = sum (l+1)^2 exp(-eps l(l+2))
PASS 1a Poisson-resummed form equals the l-sum at 50 digits for eps = 0.05, 0.18, 1/2, 1, 2, 16 pi/3 (max rel dev 1.36e-46): the pi lives in the DUAL representation only
PASS 1b CONTROL: the dual form without the curvature factor e^eps differs from the l-sum by 0.649 (test can fail)
PASS 1c small-eps series of T3: (sqrt(pi)/4) eps^(-3/2) (1 + eps + eps^2/2 + ...), i.e. a_0 = 1, a_1 = 1, a_2 = 1/2 (Gilkey, 0b); only HALF-integer powers of eps => heat-trace constant term = 0
PASS 1d Vol(S^3)/(4 pi b)^(3/2) = (sqrt(pi)/4) (L^2/b)^(3/2): the filter's pi^(-3/2) times the volume's pi^2 leaves pi^(1/2)  (half-integer parity)
PASS 1e Weyl law on S^3: coefficient omega_3 Vol/(2 pi)^3 = 1/3 (no pi); exact count/(n^3/3) at n = 4000 is 1.000375 -> 1
PASS 1f zeta_{S^3}(0) = zeta_R(-2) - 1 = -1 (zero mode removed), so the heat-trace constant term is 0 (H1)

== 2. S^4 (unit radius):  eigenvalue l(l+3), degeneracy (2l+3)(l+1)(l+2)/6;  n = l + 3/2
   minimal scalar   T4(e) ~ 149*e**2/1890 + 37*e/189 + 29/90 + 1/(3*e) + 1/(6*e**2)
   conformal scalar Tc(e) ~ -e**2/630 - 4*e/945 - 1/90 + 1/(6*e**2)
PASS 2a minimal scalar: T4 = 1/(6 e^2) + 1/(3 e) + 29/90 + O(e)  (a_0 = 1, a_1 = R/6 = 2, a_2 = 29/15 in units of L; (4 pi)^-2 Vol = 1/6)
PASS 2b conformal scalar (-Lap + R/6): Tc = 1/(6 e^2) + 0/e - 1/90 + O(e)  (a_1 = 0)
PASS 2c the same constants equal Gilkey's integrated a_2 from the curvature tensors (0b): 29/90 and -1/90 -- independent routes (spectrum vs curvature) agree
PASS 2d direct l-sum of the S^4 heat trace at e = 1e-3, 2e-3 minus the two singular terms, Richardson-extrapolated = 0.32222206 (29/90 = 0.32222222)
PASS 2e zeta(0): minimal scalar (zero mode removed) = -61/90 = 29/90 - 1 ; conformal scalar = -1/90 = -1/90  (Hurwitz continuation, exact)
PASS 2f de Rham supertrace sum_p (-1)^p Tr e^{-eps Delta_p} = 2 = chi(S^4) at eps = 0.01, 0.3, 1, 16 pi/3 (spread 0.0): the filter width drops out of the Euler characteristic
PASS 2g CONTROL: dropping the harmonic 4-form gives 1, not 2: the supertrace test can fail
PASS 2h leading behaviour Tr_p ~ C(4,p)/(6 e^2) (ranks 1,4,6,4,1): the p-form spectra used are consistent (Tr_1 * 6e^2 = 3.992 at e = 0.002)
PASS 2i (4 pi)^2 chi(S^4) = 32 pi^2 = 12 Vol(S^4_unit) = 6 x [(1/6) int R dV]: the ONLY spectral quantity equal to 32 pi^2 is this eps-independent integer times (4 pi)^2
PASS 2j Casimir-type regularised sum on S^3 (conformal scalar, omega_n = n/L): (1/2) sum n^3 e^{-lambda n} = 3/lambda^4 + 1/240 + O(lambda^2); finite part = 0.004166665675 (1/240 = 0.004166666667): RATIONAL; pi appears only if one divides by Vol(S^3) = 2 pi^2 L^3 to form a density (rho = 1/(480 pi^2 L^4), an hbar-carrying quantity)

== 3. T^3 (side 1)
PASS 3a T^3 heat trace: sum_n e^{-4 pi^2 b |n|^2} = (4 pi b)^(-3/2) theta_3(e^{-1/(4b)})^3 at 50 digits (max rel dev 1.2e-50): pi^(-3/2) prefactor, no pi in the volume

== 4. pi-parity of the normalised volume  Vol(S^d)/(4 pi)^{d/2}
   d: value   {2: (1, 'rational'), 3: (1/4, 'rational x sqrt(pi)'), 4: (1/6, 'rational'), 5: (1/32, 'rational x sqrt(pi)')}
